      "title": "Building AI That Can Reason Under Uncertainty with Thore Graepel - #786",
      "summary": "In this episode, Thore Graepel, CEO and co-founder of Metis Reasoning and Chair of Machine Learning at UCL, joins us to explore an approach to AI reasoning inspired by his work on AlphaGo. We discuss why he believes reliable reasoning requires explicit representations of knowledge and uncertainty, with language models helping to generate hypotheses and interpret evidence. Thore explains how a system could break down complex questions, gather evidence, and choose its next step based on the uncertainty it expects to resolve. We examine how this differs from agent harnesses, where smaller models and decision models might fit, and how knowledge and reasoning strategies can improve independently. We also consider the difficult judgment calls involved in assessing evidence, handling conflicting sources, and avoiding overconfidence. Finally, we discuss the limits of answer-focused benchmarks and what it would take to evaluate whether a system\u2019s reasoning genuinely supports its conclusions. \ud83d\uddd2\ufe0f Full show notes: \u2060\u2060\u2060 https://twimlai.com/go/786 .",
      "summary_html": "<p>In this episode, Thore Graepel, CEO and co-founder of Metis Reasoning and Chair of Machine Learning at UCL, joins us to explore an approach to AI reasoning inspired by his work on AlphaGo. We discuss why he believes reliable reasoning requires explicit representations of knowledge and uncertainty, with language models helping to generate hypotheses and interpret evidence. Thore explains how a system could break down complex questions, gather evidence, and choose its next step based on the uncertainty it expects to resolve. We examine how this differs from agent harnesses, where smaller models and decision models might fit, and how knowledge and reasoning strategies can improve independently. We also consider the difficult judgment calls involved in assessing evidence, handling conflicting sources, and avoiding overconfidence. Finally, we discuss the limits of answer-focused benchmarks and what it would take to evaluate whether a system\u2019s reasoning genuinely supports its conclusions.\n\n\ud83d\uddd2\ufe0f Full show notes: \u2060\u2060\u2060<a href=\"https://twimlai.com/go/786\">https://twimlai.com/go/786</a>.</p>",
